  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 309 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nStill in the upper 60s to low 70s for lows Saturday night, though \nthe much anticipated cold front creeps ever closer. By the early \nmorning hours Sunday, PoPs in west-central Missouri climb to 30-50%. \nThe front looks to move quite slowly throughout Sunday into Monday, \nallowing highs for much of the area Sunday to still reach the upper \n90s, especially in central Missouri. An extension of the Heat \nAdvisory for some of these areas may be warranted, though farther \nnorth highs may \"only\" reach the mid-90s depending on the speed of \nthe front.\n\nThe NBM has increased precipitation chances as the front moves \nthrough, with PoPs up to 60% reaching the I-44 corridor by Monday \nmorning and 50-70% chances in south-central Missouri by the \nafternoon. However, there is considerable spread in NBM members at \nthis range. There is a nearly ten degree interquartile spread in \nhigh temperatures Monday, ranging from the upper 70s to the upper \n80s in Springfield, for example. These large spreads continue for \nthe rest of the week, though the entire distribution lies in the 70s \nfor Springfield Tuesday, which is encouraging.\n\nRain comes to an end Monday night. Deterministic rainfall totals \nshow a gradient of about 0.75\" in west-central Missouri down to less \nthan a tenth of an inch in south-central Missouri. Totals are about \n0.4 to 0.2 from west to east along the I-44 corridor. The \nprobability of >0.50\" ranges from about 50% at Truman State Park to \nabout 20% in West Plains.\n\nHighs Tuesday through Thursday look to be largely below 80 degrees, \nbut again, there is very little confidence at this range. A few days \nago, highs Monday looked much lower and have only warmed the closer \nwe get, so it is best to temper our expectations for now. Still a \nslight lean toward wet and warm for the 8-14 day outlook, but no \nindication away from normal one day or the other for 3-4 weeks\nout.",
